# 🫀 Heart Disease Detection Project  
## 📊 Accuracy Comparison Report

---

# 1. Introduction
This report compares two approaches for heart disease prediction:

- Rule-Based Expert System
- Machine Learning Model (Decision Tree Classifier)

Both models were evaluated using the same dataset and feature set to ensure a fair comparison.

The goal is to evaluate the trade-off between:
- Accuracy (Machine Learning)
- Interpretability (Rule-Based System)

---

# 2. Dataset Description
The dataset contains medical attributes used for heart disease prediction:

### Features:
- cp (Chest Pain Type)
- thalach (Maximum Heart Rate Achieved)
- slope (Slope of ST segment)
- age
- sex
- thal
- ca (Number of major vessels)
- oldpeak (ST depression)
- exang (Exercise induced angina)

### Target:
- 0 → No Heart Disease  
- 1 → Heart Disease  

---

# 3. Data Preparation
- Missing values handled (drop/impute)
- Feature scaling using MinMaxScaler
- Categorical encoding using One-Hot Encoding
- Train/Test split: 80/20

---

# 4. Evaluation Metrics
Both models were evaluated using:

- Accuracy
- Precision
- Recall
- F1-score

---

# 5. Results

===== Decision Tree =====
Accuracy: 0.8097560975609757
Precision: 0.8555555555555555
Recall: 0.7475728155339806
F1-score: 0.7979274611398963

===== Expert System =====
Accuracy: 0.33658536585365856
Precision: 0.2676056338028169
Recall: 0.18446601941747573
F1-score: 0.21839080459770116
---

# 6. Analysis

## Decision Tree
- Learns patterns automatically from data
- Higher predictive performance
- Better generalization
- Less interpretable

## Expert System
- Based on human-defined rules
- Highly interpretable
- Limited flexibility
- Over-predicts positive cases

---

# 7. Explainability

### Expert System:
- Fully transparent rules
- Easy to interpret medically

### Decision Tree:
- Partially interpretable
- Can be visualized
- Learns hidden relationships

---

# 8. Limitations

### Expert System:
- Depends on manual thresholds
- Not adaptive to new data

### Decision Tree:
- Can overfit
- Less explainable than rules

---

# 9. Conclusion
The Decision Tree model outperformed the Rule-Based System in accuracy and overall performance.

However, the Expert System is more interpretable and useful for decision support.

A hybrid approach combining both methods is recommended for real-world healthcare systems.

---

# 10. Recommendation
- Use Machine Learning for prediction accuracy  
- Use Rule-Based systems for interpretability  
- Future work should explore hybrid AI models  

